WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.957
A cryptographic inventory is the foundation for harvest-now, decrypt-later mitigation.

00:00:04.957 --> 00:00:08.220
It records where cryptography is used, what it protects, who owns it,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.115
which algorithms and protocols are involved, and what dependencies affect change.

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:17.195
Inventory should include certificates, TLS endpoints, VPNs, SSH, secure email,

00:00:17.195 --> 00:00:21.560
code signing, firmware signing, key management systems, hardware security modules,

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.400
cloud services, libraries, protocols, third-party products,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.880
and vendor-managed capabilities.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.755
A useful record goes beyond naming the algorithm.

00:00:28.755 --> 00:00:32.720
It should document the business owner, technical owner, system purpose, data protected,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.260
data lifetime, exposure, key location, certificate authority, library version, platform,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.985
protocol, vendor dependency, migration status, and known blockers.

00:00:40.985 --> 00:00:43.720
This context turns inventory into decision support.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.210
It helps teams see which systems can be tested quickly and which depend on a vendor

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:47.930
roadmap,

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.650
platform upgrade, or client compatibility work.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.440
Dependency mapping is where many surprises appear.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.160
A web service may terminate TLS at a load balancer.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.450
A mobile app may pin a certificate or use an older TLS stack.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.001
A backup product may rely on a vendor-managed encryption feature.

00:01:02.001 --> 00:01:05.335
A hardware security module may support some algorithms but not others.

00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:09.200
A cloud service may hide implementation details behind a managed interface.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.735
Each dependency affects how quickly a team can reduce risk.

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:15.315
Inventory should be treated as a living program artifact.

00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:19.520
Initial discovery can come from scans, certificate management systems, cloud inventories,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.855
configuration repositories, endpoint management, network diagrams, application owners,

00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:25.380
and vendor questionnaires.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.255
The inventory will be incomplete at first, and that is normal.

00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:31.625
What matters is a repeatable process that improves coverage, tracks ownership,

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:35.880
and connects cryptographic dependencies to long-term confidentiality decisions.
